#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a88625d-af27-53c4-a457-58e65edd2cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293696} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5399f1-9fff-5cb2-8ad0-4cb50b507b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293697} {:answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293698} {:answer/id #uuid "5a252763-0942-541b-b522-67772f90d79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293699} {:answer/id #uuid "e74db172-d46a-5ed3-82af-e90331457394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc355f65-3bf9-5528-8a74-4acdf8839e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7ee43f3-f778-5c3e-9099-6bca7ddcc6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114271" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5e96422-26ae-5396-b76d-ff245e96bcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356494} {:answer/id #uuid "ed071224-8daa-5dbc-b675-93c3bf27608c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e96422-26ae-5396-b76d-ff245e96bcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356494} :answer-field/id #uuid "6646c1ae-dd3e-59d7-8786-2633e5de810b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4022d6aa-e7a1-5cc8-b441-dd8b1efe822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4022d6aa-e7a1-5cc8-b441-dd8b1efe822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356497} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cdef858-6874-5c3e-9c38-59e8a66a0ab3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54fddde1-00a5-5c23-9d33-26479c485ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54fddde1-00a5-5c23-9d33-26479c485ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c2538b3-2625-57b8-a435-029a36687462" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0866cd13-4086-562d-ae6a-193aa22c57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0866cd13-4086-562d-ae6a-193aa22c57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3a484f-84d0-5670-9864-97137baeee92" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ce94e4f-6bff-561d-bee3-cf3e011e5b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-343146" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,0),$ and $(2,4).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,),\\,(\\,-2,0\\,),\\,(\\,2,4\\,).\n$$"}]}
